Here are the three TUnit skip patterns:

**1. Skip a specific test method**

```csharp
[Test]
[Skip("Waiting on payment-gateway sandbox provisioning")]
public async Task ChargeCard_ReturnsSuccess()
{
    // ...
}
```

**2. Skip every test in an assembly (conditionally)**

```csharp
// In any file (e.g., AssemblyAttributes.cs)
#if SMOKE
[assembly: Skip("Assembly-level skip: smoke configuration — full suite disabled")]
#endif
```

Build with `-p:DefineConstants=SMOKE` to activate the skip.

**3. Runtime skip inside a test method**

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task SyncAdUsers_PullsLatestRecords()
{
    if (!IsOnCorporateVpn())
    {
        Assert.Skip("Skipped: machine is not joined to the corporate VPN");
    }

    // ... rest of test
}

private static bool IsOnCorporateVpn()
{
    // e.g., try to resolve an internal DNS name
    try
    {
        Dns.GetHostEntry("internal.corp.example.com");
        return true;
    }
    catch
    {
        return false;
    }
}
```

**Summary**

| Scope | Mechanism | When evaluated |
|---|---|---|
| Single test | `[Skip("reason")]` on method | Before test runs |
| Entire assembly | `[assembly: Skip("reason")]` | Before any test runs |
| Runtime/conditional | `Assert.Skip("reason")` | During test execution |

All three report the test as *skipped* (not failed) in the test results, with your reason message visible in the output.
